Content set beside the main flow — notes, boxed asides, and the expandable disclosure.

<section #sh-aside | `<aside>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *aside*. Compiles to eHTML `<aside>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<aside>`

<code #code:sh-aside-1>
<aside | A side note about the elephant.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-aside-1>
<aside | A side note about the elephant.>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-aside-2>
<aside type=warning .important | Be careful here.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-aside-2>
<aside type=warning .important | Be careful here.>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:sh-aside-3>
<aside type=callout |
This is a multi-line callout.

It can contain multiple paragraphs and other content like
<strong | emphasis> and inline references.
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-aside-3>
<aside type=callout |
This is a multi-line callout.

It can contain multiple paragraphs and other content like
<strong | emphasis> and inline references.
>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `note` `sidebar` `callout` `warning` `tip` `info` `caution` | Optional classification of the aside's role. Affects rendering (callouts get visual emphasis; sidebars get layout treatment). Maps to JATS via boxed-text content-type at export. |
| `title` | kwarg |  | Optional title rendered at the top of the aside (the frameable title-top convention, #31). Lifts to a <title> child tag at the normalize-to-canonical gate; the child form is equivalent. |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Optional caption rendered at the foot of the aside (frameable caption-bottom convention, #31), with the "Box N." label folded in when numbered. Lifts to a `<caption>` child tag at the gate. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `false` | Whether this aside is numbered. **Off by default** (boxed prose is usually a one-off callout). When +numbered, the aside counts in its OWN "Box N" series (the `box` counter / ref-prefix `box`), not the figure counter. |
| `+border` | boolean | default `true` | Frameable surface (#31). **On by default for boxed prose** — the visual box is the point of a callout / sidebar. Use -border to suppress the outline. |

</section>

<section #sh-note | `<note>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *note*. Compiles to eHTML `<note>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<note>`

<code #code:sh-note-1>
Some text<note | A substantive note about the text.>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-note-1>
Some text<note | A substantive note about the text.>.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-note-2>
A claim<note placement=foot | A footnote.>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-note-2>
A claim<note placement=foot | A footnote.>.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-note-3>
A definition<note placement=side | Inline-adjacent note.>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-note-3>
A definition<note placement=side | Inline-adjacent note.>.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `placement` | kwarg | `end` `foot` `side` | Determines where the note content is collected. "end" collects at article-back; "foot" collects at article-back (distinguished from "end" by CSS class on the note-list); "side" renders the content inline-adjacent to the marker. Document-wide default is "end". |
| `position` | kwarg | `end` `foot` `side` | Legacy alias for "placement" (same values, same data-note-placement output). Retained for backwards compatibility; "placement" is preferred for new documents. |
| `type` | kwarg | `substantive` `technical` `editorial` `translator` `other` | Optional classification. Most notes are substantive (authorial commentary). |

</section>

<section #sh-note-list | `<note-list>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *note-list*. Compiles to eHTML `<note-list>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<note-list>`

*No standalone example — see the registers above and the related constructs in this chapter.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `end-notes` `chapter-notes` `footnotes-collected` `other` |  |

</section>

<section #sh-endnotes | `<endnotes>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *endnotes*. Compiles to eHTML `<endnotes>`.

A placement marker: write `<endnotes>` where you want the document's collected end-notes to appear, and the note-placement plugin replaces it with the collected end-notes block (which renders as a `<note-list>`). It is the end-note counterpart to letting foot-notes collect at the page. Author it once, typically near the end of the document; the notes themselves are authored inline with `<note>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<endnotes>`

<code-block #code:sh-endnotes-1>
The claim holds<note | A note collected at the end.>.

<section | Notes>
<endnotes>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-endnotes-1>
The claim holds<note | A note collected at the end.>.

<section | Notes>
<endnotes>
</minipage>

*The inline `<note>` collects at the `<endnotes>` marker instead of at the article back's default position.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

`<endnotes>` takes no attributes. It is a marker — its content is generated (the collected notes), not authored.

</section>

<section #sh-details | `<details>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *details*. Compiles to eHTML `<details>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<details>`

<code-block #code:sh-details-1>
<details>
  <summary | More background>
  Additional context for the curious reader. The summary is the
  visible heading; this body shows when the disclosure is opened.
</details>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-details-1>
<details>
  <summary | More background>
  Additional context for the curious reader. The summary is the
  visible heading; this body shows when the disclosure is opened.
</details>
</minipage>

*The canonical shape: a &lt;summary&gt; for the visible heading, followed by the disclosure body. The body is recursively parsed as prose / block content.*

<code-block #code:sh-details-2>
<details +open>
  <summary | Always-expanded section>
  This disclosure is expanded by default.
</details>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-details-2>
<details +open>
  <summary | Always-expanded section>
  This disclosure is expanded by default.
</details>
</minipage>

*The +open boolean kwarg expands the disclosure by default. Maps to HTML's standard open attribute.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `open` | kwarg | `true` `false` | HTML's open attribute on <details>. When present, the disclosure is expanded by default. Either +open or open=true form works; absence (or -open / open=false) renders collapsed. |

</section>

<section #sh-summary | `<summary>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *summary*. Compiles to eHTML `<summary>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<summary>`

<code #code:sh-summary-1>
<summary | More background>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-summary-1>
<summary | More background>
</minipage>

*The visible heading of a &lt;details&gt; disclosure. Appears as a child of &lt;details&gt;.*

<code #code:sh-summary-2>
<summary | Click to reveal the <em | hidden> details>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-summary-2>
<summary | Click to reveal the <em | hidden> details>
</minipage>

*Inline markup in a summary. The recursive-content pass parses the pipe content normally.*

</section>
